# Per-advisory suppressions for the dependency audit gate.
#
# Read by `npm run check:audit`, which is the CI gate — see
# app/scripts/audit-allowlist-core.ts for the mechanism and CONTRIBUTING.md
# "Dependency audit gate" for the procedure that leads here.
#
# This file is the "third option" CONTRIBUTING.md used to say did not exist.
# `npm audit` is pass/fail with no per-advisory escape hatch, so an advisory with
# no published fix took the gate red and kept it there, and a red gate on a base
# branch is inherited by every open Dependabot PR — which is exactly the state in
# which a real regression on one of them gets waved through as "the usual red".
#
# IT IS DELIBERATELY SHAPED LIKE .trivyignore, down to `exp:`, because the
# decision is the same decision and should not have to be learned twice. An entry
# here means "we know, and we have decided to ship anyway". That needs to be
# reviewable, so every entry must carry:
#
#   1. the advisory ID
#   2. why it does not apply, or why the fix cannot be taken yet
#   3. an expiry date — the suppression stops working ON that date
#
# Format:
#   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#   # <why this is suppressed, and what unblocks removing it>
#
# ONE ENTRY IS ONE ADVISORY, NOT ONE PACKAGE. npm reports a package as vulnerable
# both when an advisory names it and when it only depends on something
# vulnerable. An entry clears the advisory everywhere it is reported, including
# every package that merely inherits it, and a *new* advisory against any of
# those packages still goes red. So the braces entry this file first held was
# one line for eight reported packages, and it was not a blanket over those
# eight.
#
# THE RUNTIME GATE NEEDS AN EXPLICIT MARKER. A bare entry covers the build-time
# gate only. To suppress something in the tree that ships inside the image, the
# entry must end in `runtime`:
#
#   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx exp:YYYY-MM-DD runtime
#
# This is the one place the file does NOT follow .trivyignore, on purpose. That
# file backs four scans from one list and its header warns that an entry
# therefore widens to images that never reported the finding. The same widening
# here would cross the line CONTRIBUTING.md draws between the two gates — runtime
# red means the advisory ships to users, build-time red means it is confined to
# the toolchain — and that is too big a difference to cross by omission. Every
# entry this file has ever held is dev-tree; if one ever needs `runtime`, that
# fact should be legible on the line itself.
#
# Prefer bumping, then an `overrides` entry, over a line here — that is
# CONTRIBUTING.md's order and this is its last resort, not its first. Issue #194
# is the cautionary case: it sat open for months on the belief that the eslint dev
# tree needed eslint@10, when four of its HIGH advisories were cleared by a plain
# lockfile refresh. Check `npm view <pkg> versions` against the advisory's
# affected range before concluding there is no fix.
#
# NOTHING REMOVES AN ENTRY WHEN THE ADVISORY IS FIXED UPSTREAM. It simply stops
# being reported and the line stays, inert, until someone deletes it — `exp:`
# stops a suppression working, it does not delete anything. `npm run check:audit`
# ends by naming the entries that matched nothing, as warnings, never failures,
# for the reason scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh gives: a stale entry is not
# a vulnerability, and a gate that goes red for tidiness is a gate people stop
# reading. Unlike that script, this one is trustworthy locally — it reads
# package-lock.json, which is the same file CI reads, not a base image whose
# cached layer may be weeks stale.
#
# Populate only from real findings, never pre-emptively.

